                <pb facs="0253" n="133" sameAs="frus:frus1922v01/pg133"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">800.30/8a: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Ambassador in Great
                        Britain</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Harvey</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1921-12-16T16:00:00-05:00"><hi rendition="#italic">December 16,
                                1921—4 p.m.</hi></date></dateline></opener>
                <p>685. I understand that Briand is to visit Lloyd George tomorrow. Please arrange
                    to deliver to him the following personal message:</p>
                <p>[Here follows the message to Briand as quoted in telegram number 552, December
                    16, to the Ambassador in France, printed <hi rendition="#italic"
                    >supra.</hi>]</p>
                <p>[Paraphrase.] This matter is of utmost importance, as position taken by France
                    may destroy work of Conference. We are informed Great Britain will not consent
                    to agreement on present basis if France persists in being free to construct 10
                    new ships. Great Britain, Japan, and Italy are agreed that 175,000 tons of
                    capital ships is a fair allowance for France. Italy desires equality with France
                    in this regard but is satisfied with the above agreement. [End paraphrase.]</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hughes</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
